# A report on the first landing on the Moon from three sources: the mission's own air-to-ground
# transcript (a work of the United States government), and a newspaper, The Daily Lantern, and an
# encyclopaedia entry, both written for this lesson. Notes, a simple and a full plan, a list of
# sources, and the report itself.
lesson writing-a-report-from-three-sources v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=8 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A report from three sources"
  goal "Write a report from three sources: take notes, check where they agree, plan it, and name the sources."

  look {
    scene 36x25 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row top space=3 align=end {
          sourcecard card ask=0
          loosepages pages count=4
        }
        timeline line from=16 to=24 step=2 unit="July" years=[16, 20, 24] labels=["Launch", "Landing", "Home"]
      }
    }
    say """
      A report tells what is known about something, and a good one is built from more than one
      source. Check who made each source and whether they were there: a record made at the time
      is first-hand. Take notes of key words only, see where two sources agree, and put the notes
      under the headings of a plan. Write in your own words, and list your sources at the end.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.report-note k=0
    }
    show writing.report-first-hand k=0 hard-k=1
    show writing.report-note k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
    show writing.report-which-source k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    level medium hard {
      show writing.report-note k=2 hard-k=3
    }
    level easy {
      show writing.report-first-hand k=2
    }
    show writing.report-which-source k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    level hard {
      show writing.report-which-source k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show writing.report-plan-order k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show writing.report-plan-under k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show writing.report-plan-under k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.report-why-source k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.report-write k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Notes in key words, facts checked in two sources, a plan of headings, your own words, and the sources named."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a report is made, not copied: facts are gathered from several sources, noted in
    key words, checked where two sources agree, sorted under the headings of a plan, and written in
    the child's own words with the sources named. The way in judges from a source card whether its
    maker was there. The core picks the best note of a sentence, finds which source gives a fact or
    whether both do, puts the report's headings in order (a simple plan) and places notes under
    them (a full plan). The wrong idea to watch for is copying: a note that is the whole sentence,
    or a report sentence lifted from a source. If the child is stuck, cover the source and ask them
    to say the fact to you, then write down only the words they needed. Off the page, choose a
    topic of their own and find three sources for it in the library, noting who made each one. The
    Daily Lantern and the encyclopaedia entry were written for this lesson and are not real
    publications; their facts are Apollo 11's (launched 16 July 1969, the Eagle down in the Sea of
    Tranquillity on 20 July, Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin outside for about two and a half hours
    while Michael Collins stayed in orbit, home to the Pacific on 24 July), and Armstrong's words
    are as NASA's transcript prints them. China's standard for grades five and six (第三学段, 梳理与探究
    3) asks children to 尝试写简单的研究报告, to try writing a simple research report.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a note is made once in the teacher's pen first, the sources to judge are the transcript and then the encyclopaedia, the notes drop a plain fact, the facts to find sit in one source or plainly in both, the plan's notes are the crew and the splashdown, and the two-star is the newspaper against the transcript."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the source to judge is the newspaper written on the day, the notes must turn yesterday into a date, the facts to find are said in different words in each source, there is one more, the plan's notes tempt the wrong heading, the two-star is copying without naming the source, and the three-star is the whole report."
  }
}
